# In Situ Fabrication of Metal–Organic Framework Thin Films with Enhanced Pervaporation Performance

## abstract
The intrinsic porosity in the periodic structures of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) endows them with a great potential for membrane separation. However, facile fabrication of crystalline MOF membranes has been challenging and limited to few materials for economic and environmental considerations. Herein, a continuous Zr‐MOF thin film with a thickness of ≈180 nm has been fabricated via in situ recrystallization of MOF nanoparticles on the porous support under formic acid vapor. Owing to the inherent microporosity and the well‐established hydrophilicity during membrane fabrication, the MOF thin films exhibit excellent pervaporation performance with separation factors of 2630, 501 and fluxes of 1.45, 1.41 kg m−2 h−1) for n‐butanol dehydration and methanol/methyl tert‐butyl ether (MeOH/MTBE) separation, respectively. The structural stability of the film has been further confirmed by its steady performance in the 10‐day pervaporation test. This in situ recrystallization method induced by a trace amount of acid vapor with no extra ingredients opens a new avenue for the facile membrane fabrication of various MOF materials to feasibly realize their versatile potential as membrane materials.

The intrinsic porosity in the periodic structures of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) endows them with a great potential for membrane separation. However, facile fabrication of crystalline MOF membranes has been challenging and limited to few materials for economic and environmental considerations. Herein, a continuous Zr-MOF thin film with a thickness of $\approx180\ \mathsf{n m}$ has been fabricated via in situ recrystallization of MOF nanoparticles on the porous support under formic acid vapor. Owing to the inherent microporosity and the well-established hydrophilicity during membrane fabrication, the MOF thin films exhibit excellent pervaporation performance with separation factors of 2630, 501 and fluxes of 1.45, $\mathsf{1.47~k g~m^{-2}~h^{-1}})$ for $\pmb{n}$ -butanol dehydration and methanol/methyl tert-butyl ether (MeOH/MTBE) separation, respectively. The structural stability of the film has been further confirmed by its steady performance in the 10-day pervaporation test. This in situ recrystallization method induced by a trace amount of acid vapor with no extra ingredients opens a new avenue for the facile membrane fabrication of various MOF materials to feasibly realize their versatile potential as membrane materials.
## 1. Introduction
Membrane separation has been demonstrated as an attractive alternative to conventional energy-intensive separation processes due to enhanced selectivity, energy-efficiency, and eco-friendliness. Combining membrane separation and vaporization, pervaporation (PV)[1] has been considered an effective technique for the separation of liquid mixtures in replacement of energy-consuming distillation, especially for azeotropic mixtures. To achieve superior separation with large fluxes, various membrane materials have been investigated involving organic polymers,[2] zeolites,[3] metal– organic frameworks (MOFs),[4] covalent organic frameworks (COFs),[5] polymers of intrinsic microporosity (PIMs),[6] and Mxenes.[7] However, polymer-based organic membranes could suffer from poor stability for organic liquid components while the restricted pore diameter of zeolites limits their potentials. In addition, the strong permeability-selectivity trade-off of target compounds remains unsolved for conventional polymer-based membranes.[8]
As an extensive class of porous crystalline materials built from dynamic coordination bonds between metal nodes and multitopic organic ligands, MOFs have been dramatically developed by reticular chemistry with unique features including versatile topologies, customizable pore diameter, and tailorable skeleton.[9] A wide range of research has demonstrated MOFs to be feasible for membrane fabrication.[4b,10] Among the investigations of pervaporation separation, MOF materials have been extensively investigated for pure MOF membranes[11] or as fillers of mixed matrix membranes[12] for organic liquid dehydration, removal or recovery of organics from aqueous solutions, and separation of organic mixtures, mostly on the well-known ZIF-8, UiO-66 and its derivatives, MIL-53, etc.[4b]
Although these membranes exhibited significant potentials, fabrication of high-performance MOF membranes has always been challenging due to the sophisticated preparation involving complex nucleation and growth of MOFs, especially oriented MOF thin films.[13] Through immersing the (seeded) supports in synthetic solution, the traditional in situ solvothermal synthesis and secondary growth methods can hardly achieve ultrathin MOF films with a particular orientation due to the unmanageable nucleation and crystal growth. Besides, a large number of extra reagents are required during the process with unavoidable homogeneous nucleation, causing chemical waste and potential environmental issues. Although the layer-by-layer method can be expected to control the thickness and the orientation of thin films, the time-consuming and labor-intensive process is not well accepted in the practical industry.[14] Li et al. introduced a solvothermal route to tailor the crystal size and morphology of ZIF-7 and to manipulate the orientation of MOF films through evolutionary selection in a van der Drift– type growth.[15] Recently, Wei et al. established a carbon nanotube-induced approach to fabricating an oriented ZIF-8 membrane.[16] Pioneering work of solvent-free fabrication of MOF membranes was conducted where zinc precursor was deposited on the support followed by ligand-vapor treatment to achieve ZIF-8 thin films.[17] Compared with conventional solvothermal strategies, solvent-free fabrication is of great interest as an easyto-operate, low-capital-cost, and eco-friendly method to fabricate MOF thin films. However, the investigations have been limited to ZIF-8 with a vaporizable ligand and have barely been reported on other MOF families.
$\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ -based MOFs are constructed by hexanuclear zirconium clusters $(\mathrm{Zr}_{6})$ coordinated by multitopic carboxylate ligands, where the high valence of zirconium endows $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs with strong robustness and the structural versatility of the building blocks induces a myriad of Zr-MOFs with distinct topology and properties.[18] The robustness of ${\tt Z r-M O F}$ membranes has been demonstrated by the extensive investigation of UiO-66 with superior membrane separation performance.[19] Thanks to the dynamic nature of the coordinative connection, modulators like monocarboxylic acids have been usually employed for coordinative competition with the bridging ligands and $\mathsf{p H}$ adjustment to manipulate the crystallization process for a particular phase from other competing phases.[20] As a $\mathrm{Zr_{6}}$ -based MOF, the scu-net NU-906 was composed of 8-connected $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ cluster and tetratopic 1,4-dibromo-2,3,5,6-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene $\left(\mathrm{TCPB}{\cdot}\mathrm{Br}_{2}\right)$ linker,[21] the crystallization of which could be modulated by the acidic modulator, resulting in a phase-pure product with microporous channels suitable for molecular sieving. Inspired by the dynamic connection of MOFs’ skeleton and the modulated synthesis of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF, we proposed that acid vapor could induce recrystallization fabrication of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF membranes in a controllable way.
Herein, $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}_{6}}$ -based NU-906, which has yet to be investigated as membrane material, was deposited on the porous support and introducing a trace amount of formic acid vapor induced the formation of continuous pinhole-free MOF thin films with preferred orientation in a fashion of in situ recrystallization. The prepared MOF thin films exhibited excellent pervaporation performance and long-term stability for different azeotropic components, including $n$ -butanol/water, ethanol/water, and methanol/methyl tert-butyl ether (MeOH/MTBE).
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Preparation of MOF Nanoparticles
NU-906 nanoparticles with scu topology (Figure 1a,c) were solvothermally constructed with tetratopic TCPB- $\cdot\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ linkage and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ cluster from N, N-dimethylformamide with benzoic acid as synthetic modulator at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Its phase purity can be demonstrated by the well-matching of its experimental and simulated powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Figure  1b). Ellipsoidal morphology was observed with a size of $200{\sim}300~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  1c; Figure S2, Supporting Information). $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm measurement under 77 K demonstrated a Brunauer– Emmett–Teller (BET) area of $744~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with ${\approx}1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ pore size calculated by the density functional theory (DFT) method (Figure  1d,e). The high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image along with the electron diffraction pattern (Figure  1f) indicated the crystallinity of the nanoparticles with a lattice spacing of ${\approx}1.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ which was consistent with the periodicity of the structure.
## 2.2. In Situ Fabrication of MOF Thin Films
In this research, $350\mathrm{~nm~}\mathrm{{s}}$ töber silica particles were compacted into a porous silica disk with a diameter of $22~\mathrm{mm}$ and a thickness of $2~\mathrm{mm}$ , which was further calcined and polished. Before loading NU-906 nanoparticles, a layer of $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ Stöber silica particles was deposited on the support to generate a smoother surface for thin film assembly. The suspension of $10~\mathrm{\mg}$ NU-906 nanoparticles in 2.6 mL N, N-dimethylformamide was wet-coated on the prepared porous silica support with a thickness of around $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Scheme  1; Figure S3a,b, Supporting Information).
MOF-loaded support was then transferred into the Teflonlined stainless steel autoclave and placed on a Teflon holder while a certain amount of formic acid was included in the liner. The MOF thin film formed in the sealed autoclave at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ where formic acid was continuously vaporized to facilitate the recrystallization of MOF films on the porous substrate (Scheme 1).
Under the autogenous vapor of ${80\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ formic acid at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the MOF thin film gradually formed, the crystallinity of which was recorded by X-ray diffraction patterns (Figure 2a) where characteristic peaks corresponding to NU-906 were weak after $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ and intensified with the prolonged time. Compared with the diffraction pattern of bulk NU-906, an obviously higher intensity of the (100), (101) and (001) facets at $5^{\circ}$ in the pattern of MOF thin film suggested the preferred orientation of NU-906 on the substrate. After $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ of treatment by formic acid vapor, NU-906 remained isolated as the nanoparticles loaded on the support at the beginning (Figure  2b). With the time prolonged to $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , NU-906 particles merged together in a noncontinuous way (Figure 2c). After $^{15\mathrm{h}}$ , a film was achieved despite of the pinholes on the surface (Figure 2d). Until $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ , a dense and continuous MOF thin film was observed covering the porous silica support with high crystallinity (Figure  2a,e). The cross-section view demonstrated the continuity of the film with a thickness of ${\approx}180~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2f). In addition, it was observed that the roughness of the NU-906 thin film gradually decreased to $1.34\ \mathrm{nm}$ from 8 to $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Figure S4, Supporting Information), which further indicated that extending the acid vapor treatment can achieve a smoother film surface. The cross-section of the film was further attained by the focused ion beam (FIB) technique where the spatial distribution of silica, carbon, bromine, and zirconium observed by annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscope (ADF-STEM) indicated the upper MOF film, the lower silica support, and the interface (Figure  2g). Through this in situ recrystallization process induced by formic acid vapor, a thin and continuous NU-906 film can be feasibly achieved by steaming the MOF nanoparticles for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Apart from the convenience of fabrication, this method can be very eco-friendly since no solvent, extra building block supplies, and reagents are involved during the fabrication process except for a trace amount of formic acid.
A transmission electron microscope (TEM) was employed to observe the exfoliated NU-906 membrane where the electron diffraction pattern of the selected area confirmed the thin film to be the NU-906 phase with a lattice spacing of ${\approx}1.7~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  3a–c). Even though MOF thin films can be sensitive to the electron beams, the HRTEM image was attained with characteristic lattice fringes of NU-906 for (100), (101) and (001)
facets with a lattice spacing of $1.7\mathrm{nm}$ , suggesting the preferred film formation along the ac plane. The exfoliated fragment was further characterized by an atomic force microscope (AFM) where a step-and-terrace structure was observed at the edge of the surface, as shown in Figure 4a and Figure S5 (Supporting Information). Each terrace was ${\approx}3.6$ nm high and it can be accordingly concluded that the $180\mathrm{nm}$ -thick MOF thin film was stacked by around 50 terraces according to the cross-section of MOF thin film (Figure  4a and  2f). In addition, it can be reasonably deduced that each terrace is composed of three layers of (010) plane, suggesting a preferred $^{b}$ orientation (Figure S6, Supporting Information).[21a] However, it was difficult to identify the ordered angstrom-sized channels from the high-resolution AFM tomographic images (Figure S5, Supporting Information), which could be due to the structural defects induced by coordinated formate groups and the reticular-merohedral twin scu topology of NU-906.[21a]
To further realize the film formation process, the MOF films were exfoliated from the support for Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy. As MOF thin film gradually grew on the support till $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ , IR bands at 2950 and $2875~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ emerged which were assigned as the stretching vibration of $\mathrm{C-H}$ in the ligated formate (Figure  3d). The bands at 1608 and $1410~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with their shoulders can be attributed to the antisymmetric, symmetric stretching of the carboxylic group and the $\mathrm{C-H}$ bending in the structure of formate. As a result, we concluded that more formate coordinated onto the MOF structure during the in situ formation of the thin film under formic acid vapor. Likewise, proton NMR evaluation of digested thin film fabricated in $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ indicated an elevated amount of formate group at a chemical shift of $8.25\mathrm{ppm}$ , where around 6.6 formate groups were determined per $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}_{6}}$ cluster by integration (Figure  3e). The formate amount in the structure exceeded the theoretical coordinationaccessible sites for the 8-connected $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ cluster in NU-906, suggesting potential structural defects that can be favorable for particular substance-membrane interactions.
Formic acid addition of $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ was further investigated where the NU-906-loaded support was surrounded by more concentrated formic acid vapor for the formation of NU-906 films. With $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ formic acid introduced into the system for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ , some gaps between silica nanoparticles have been filled by recrystallized NU-906 nanoparticles under formic acid vapor, even though the formed film was discontinuous and some isolated NU-906 nanoparticles existed on the surface of the film, similar to the morphology observed at low acid dosage (Figure  5a). As the time extended to $12\mathrm{~h~}$ , a continuous film with no obvious gaps or pinholes formed on the support with a thickness of ${\approx}180~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 5b,e,h). The XRD pattern indicated its good crystallinity where the peaks at $5^{\circ}$ corresponding to the (100), (101) and (001) planes intensified meanwhile the peaks at 7 and 8.74 disappeared for the (010) and (110) (111) (011) planes, suggesting the preferred film formation along the ac plane (Figure 5g). As the treatment time extended to $15\mathrm{-}18\mathrm{h}$ , a dense and continuous NU-906 thin film was obtained with good crystallinity and a thickness of $200\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 5c,d,f ). The cross-section of the film was further attained by the focused ion beam (FIB) technique where the spatial distribution of silica, carbon, bromine, and zirconium was observed by ADF-STEM (Figure  5h). Through increasing the formic acid addition, less fabrication time was required to achieve a dense and continuous MOF thin film and more acid vapor favored the orientation preference of the films. The exfoliated fragment of the thin film was additionally characterized by an atomic force microscope (AFM) where a similar step-and-terrace structure as the MOF film fabricated under a lower amount of formic acid was observed at the edge of the surface as shown in Figure 4b. Each terrace was ${\approx}3.6$ nm high which was composed of three layers of (010) plane, further suggesting a $\boldsymbol{b}$ -oriented film (Figure S6, Supporting Information). In addition, it can be accordingly concluded that the $200~\mathrm{nm}$ -thick MOF thin film was stacked by around 55 terraces according to the cross-section of MOF thin film (Figure 4b and 5f).
## 2.3. Pervaporation Performance of the NU-906 Films
The water contact angle of the MOF thin films was measured to identify their hydrophilicity change during the fabrication process (Figure 6a). When a droplet of water was brought in contact with the surface of the porous silica support, the water contact angle appeared to be $174^{\circ}$ , which was attributed to the hydrophobic nature of silica. As NU-906 nanoparticles were loaded on the silica support, the water contact angle remarkably decreased to around $90^{\circ}$ , indicating the higher hydrophilicity of NU-906 than silica. During the MOF film fabrication process induced by formic acid vapor, gradually reduced water contact angles from $90^{\circ}$ to $12^{\circ}$ were observed with time prolonged from 8 to $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ , suggesting steadily improved hydrophilicity of the thin film which could be attributed to the increased structural defects and elevated amount of formate in the framework as demonstrated by the FTIR and proton NMR characterization (Figure 3d,e).
In industry, separating azeotropic mixtures was commonly achieved through energy-intensive distillation, such as alcohol dehydration and MeOH/MTBE separation. Taking advantage of the distinct interfacial interaction and size exclusion effect, membrane-based pervaporation has emerged as a promising alternative. Considering the distinct pore size and good hydrophilicity, the NU-906 films in this study were evaluated for the pervaporated separation of $90\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ ethanol/water at $76~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ $90\ \mathrm{wt.}\%$ $n$ -butanol/water at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 20  wt. $\%$ MeOH/MTBE at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively.
As for alcohol dehydration, ethanol and $n$ -butanol were readily retained by the NU-906 films which could be explained by their bulkier molecule sizes than water molecules as well as the excellent hydrophilicity of the MOF films. As the fabrication time increased from 12 to $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ , gradually enhanced alcohol/water separation factors were observed from 102 to 142 for ethanol/water mixture and from 1312 to 2630 for $n$ -butanol/ water mixture, respectively. For the thin film prepared for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ with ${80}~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ formic acid, a permeation flux of $0.65\ \mathrm{kg\m^{-2}\ h^{-1}}$ was observed with an ethanol/water separation factor of 142 (Figure  S7 and Table S2, Supporting Information) while a much higher $n$ -butanol/water separation factor of 2630 was achieved with a permeation flux of $1.45\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ . Compared with the membrane performance in previous literature, the NU-906 thin films in this study outperform most of representative membranes for $n$ -butanol dehydration (Figure  6c; Table S3, Supporting Information). The improved $n$ -butanol/water pervaporation performance reported here was attributed to not only the high hydrophilicity but also the precise water molecular recognition through the microporous channels of NU-906 thin film. Moreover, the NU-906 film exhibited good mecha­ nical robustness and long-term stability confirmed by the steady permeation flux and the high $n$ -butanol/water separation factor in a 10-day pervaporation test (Figure 6d). After 4 hours’ pervaporation with this film, the $n$ -butanol can be concentrated up to $99.3\pm0.2~\mathrm{wt.\%}$ in the feed side. A larger MOF loading of $1.16\mathrm{\mg\NU{-}}906$ particles on the porous $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ support has been further attempted for the film fabrication where a thicker $(1\upmu\mathrm m$ , Figure S8, Supporting Information) NU-906 membrane was obtained which exhibited a reduced flux of $0.11\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with a separation factor of 244 for ethanol dehydration.
To further determine the size sieving effect, the prepared MOF thin films were evaluated for the pervaporation of another challenging azeotropic mixture of 20  wt. $\%$ MeOH/ MTBE, where gradually improved separation factors from 299 to 501 with simultaneously elevated flux from 0.9 to $1.41\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ were observed with the synthetic time ranging from 12 to $^{18\mathrm{h}}$ . Compared with previously reported materials, a superior $\mathrm{MeOH/MTBE}$ separation factor of 501 with a high flux of $\mathsf{L}.41\ \mathrm{kg}/(\mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{h})$ was achieved for the NU-906 film fabricated in $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ with ${80}~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ formic acid addition (Figure  6e; Table S4, Supporting Information). The stability test for MeOH/MTBE separation further proved the robust structure of NU-906 film (Figure  6f). It could be speculated that the inorganic–organic hybrid network renders the MOF thin film with extreme robustness for MeOH/MTBE system compared with organicnature polymeric membranes. After 4  hours’ pervaporation, $99.2\pm0.2\ \mathrm{wt.\%}$ methanol can be achieved in the permeate side while MTBE has been concentrated to $97.3\mathrm{\wt.\%}$ in the feed side. The intrinsic ${\approx}1\mathrm{nm}$ pore size of NU-906 endows the MOF membrane with the ability to readily distinguish MeOH out of sizable MTBE. The excellent MeOH/MTBE separation factor and the long-term stability demonstrated their potentials in the practical applications (Figure 6e,f). The facile fabrication as well as the superior performance can make a critical difference to the MeOH/MTBE separation industry.
A schematic description of the diameters of molecules to be separated and their diffusion behavior across the MOF thin film was illustrated in Figure 6b where water and MeOH molecules were preferentially adsorbed on the hydrophilic surface followed by a faster diffusion through the rigid angstromsized channels compared with their bulky alcohol and MTBE pair molecules. Unfortunately, lower pervaporation fluxes were observed for the more oriented films formed under the $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ acid addition. It can be speculated that more concentrated acid vapor could achieve a more cross-linked film, leading to reduced fluxes. In addition, the separation factors observed from the more oriented film under $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ acid addition are close to those from the films under ${80\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ formic acid addition, which could be attributed to the tortuous transport pathway in the film fabricated under ${80\ \upmu\mathrm{L}}$ acid compared to the oriented film with $1\mathrm{nm}$ microchannels fabricated under $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ acid.
## 3. Conclusion
In this study, a dense and continuous Zr-MOF thin film with intrinsic microporous channels was readily fabricated through recrystallization of the MOF nanoparticles on the substrate, induced by a trace amount of formic acid vapor with no extra ingredients. Compared with the conventional methods, the vapor-induced in situ recrystallization method can be convenient, eco-friendly, and low-cost, developing an intriguing method for oriented ultrathin MOF film fabrication. The prepared thin films in this research exhibited stable high-separation performance toward azeotropic mixtures with separation factors of 2630 and 501, and fluxes of 1.45 and $1.41\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ for $n$ -butanol/water and MeOH/MTBE, respectively. Our approach developed an avenue for the fabrication of highly selective MOF thin film for the potential separation of azeotropic mixtures.
## 4. Experimental Section
Starting Materials: Zirconium chloride $(\geq99.5\%)$ , formic acid $(\geq96\%)$ , $p$ -tolylmagnesium bromide solution ( $\mathsf{l}.0\mathsf{M}$ in DMF), hexabromobenzene $(98\%)$ , hexabromobenzene $(97\%)$ , bromine (reagent grade), carbon tetrachloride $(99.9\%)$ , benzoic acid $(\geq99\%)$ and ammonium hydroxide $(N H_{4}O H$ , $28\mathrm{-}30~\mathrm{wt.}\%)$ were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich. Ethanol (AR), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ , AR), $n$ -butanol $(99.8\%)$ , methanol $(99.8\%)$ , methyl tert-butyl ether (MTBE, $99.5\%$ ) and tetrahydrofuran (THF) were purchased from Tianjin Yuanli Chemical Co., Ltd. Acetone (AR) and chloroform $(99.8\%)$ were purchased from Tianjin Jiangtian Chemical Technology Co., Ltd. All the reagents were analytical grade and have not been further purified. Deionized water was utilized for the entire experiment.
Ligand Synthesis: Hexabromobenzene $(5.0\textrm{\textit{g}},9.07\textrm{\textit{m n}}$ ol) and $p$ -tolylmagnesium bromide $(7~\mathsf{M}$ in THF, $700~\mathsf{m m o l}.$ ) were added to the flask under nitrogen. The mixture was stirred for $\rceil5\textrm{h}$ at ambient temperature and the color of the final mixture was gray. And then the reaction was placed into the ice bath. A mixture combined with $7m L$ bromine and $60~\mathsf{m L}$ of ${\mathsf{C C l}}_{4}$ was placed in a funnel and then added dropwise to the reaction mixture. The reaction was stirred at ambient temperature under nitrogen for $\mathsf{1.5h}$ . After the reaction, the mixture was poured into ice, and HCl aqueous solution $(6\mathrm{~M},50\mathrm{~mL})$ was added. The solid product was filtered off and washed with methanol. The obtained solid (1  g) was placed in a $700~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon liner with $12~\mathsf{m L}$ water and $3m L$ concentrated $\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ . The Teflon liner was placed in stainless steel autoclave and heated at $180~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The final product was filtered off and washed with $200\ m\mathsf{L}\mathsf{T H F/C H C l_{3}}$ (7:3). The purity of the ligand was evaluated by the proton Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (1H NMR) characterization (Figure S1, Supporting Information).
Synthesis of NU-906 Powder: Zirconium chloride $(58.5~\mathrm{~}\mathsf{\xi}\mathsf{m g},$ 0.251  mmol) and benzoic acid $(24\mathrm{~mg},\ 0.797\mathrm{~mmol})$ ) were dissolved into $N$ , $N$ -dimethylformamide $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{1.8~\mL}})$ to form the $Z r_{6}$ cluster by subsequently heating the mixture at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $\texttt{l h}$ . TCPB- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\sf B r}_{2}$ linker $(36~\mathrm{~mg},~0.0503~\mathrm{~mmol})$ was dissolved into N, $N$ -dimethylformamide $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{l}}.8\:\mathsf{m L})$ , then added into the $Z\mathsf{r}_{6}$ cluster solution. Benzoic acid ( $\boldsymbol{288}\mathrm{mg}$ $2.36\ m m^{}$ ) was added to the mixture which was then heated at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight (15–16 h). The white powder was centrifuged out and washed with $N$ , N-dimethylformamide $(3\times20~\mathsf{m L})$ and acetone $(3\times20~\mathsf{m L})$ , respectively. After being soaked in acetone overnight, the NU-906 powder was additionally washed with acetone $(3\times20~\mathsf{m L})$ prior to being dried under vacuum at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $\rceil\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ and placed in a desiccator.
Synthesis of Stöber Silica Particles: Stöber silica particles with sizes of $350\ n m$ were synthesized according to classical procedures with modifications to achieve a narrow particle size distribution.[22] Tetraethyl orthosilicate (TEOS), ammonia solution, DI water, and ethanol were mixed in appropriate compositions as listed in Table S1 (Supporting Information), called solution A and B, respectively. For synthesizing $350~\mathsf{n m}$ Stöber silica, solution B was added to the conical flask followed by ${\approx}70$ min of stirring. And then, solution A was quickly added to solution B (in a conical flask). This reaction continued for $2\ h$ at room temperature under stirring at 400  rpm. After synthesis, particles were centrifuged at 9000 rpm for 15 min and washed with $40~\mathsf{m L}$ ethanol for four times. Particles were dried at $70~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight and then calcined at $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $4\ h$ with a heating rate of $2\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\ \mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ and cooling rate of $4^{\circ}\mathsf C\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ .
Preparation of Porous $S i O_{2}$ Support (Stöber Silica Disk): Stöber silica disks were prepared according to the method described by Pham et al.[23] In short, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{1.0~mL}}$ aqueous solution of ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S i O}}_{3}$ $0.5\%$ in DI water) was added dropwise into $\rceil0\ \mathrm{g}$ of $350~\mathsf{n m}$ Stöber silica particles and the particles were ground for 10 min in an agate mortar. Then, $\mathsf{1.6}\mathsf{g}$ particles were compacted in a die at the pressure of $160\ \mathrm{kg}\mathsf{f}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ for $60~\mathsf{s}$ The compacted disks were calcined at $950~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $2\ h$ with a heating rate of $100^{\circ}C\ h^{-1}$ . The diameter and thickness of the porous silica disk were about 22 and $2\mathsf{m m}$ , respectively. The porosity measured by a mercury porosimeter was $35\%$ with an average pore size of $\mathsf{109n m}$ . After calcination, the disks were manually polished with SiC sandpaper (grit 1000 and 2000).
Preparation of MOF Thin Film: Specific amount of NU-906 powder $(70m g)$ was dispersed into $2.6~\textsf{m L}~N$ , N-dimethylformamide. The NU-906/DMF dispersion $(200~\upmu\updownarrow,~0.77~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{M O F})$ was assembled on the surface of porous $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ support by wet-coating. The MOFloaded support was placed on a Teflon holder in a $50~\mathsf{m L}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave. A certain amount of formic acid (80, $250~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was added to the bottom of the Teflon liner and the autoclave was subsequently sealed. After heating at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $8\mathrm{-}78\mathrm{~h~}$ , the MOF thin films formed on the surface of the substrate, which was washed with $N$ , $N$ -dimethylformamide, dried in a vacuum oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ overnight and preserved in a desiccator until pervaporation test.
Pervaporation Test: Pervaporation performance of the MOF thin film was evaluated by home-made equipment (Figure S9, Supporting Information) where the azeotropic mixtures were heated at $76~^{\circ}C$ for ethanol/water solution, $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $90\ \mathrm{wt.}\%$ $n$ -butanol/water, and $30~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 20  wt. $\%$ MeOH/MTBE, respectively. The permeate was collected in a cold trap at 60–120  min intervals. The total flux $(1,\ k g\ m^{-2}\ \mathsf{h}^{-1})$ and separation factor $(\alpha)$ are defined as follows:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\displaystyle{J=\frac{W}{A\times\mathrm{t}}}}\\ {\displaystyle{\alpha_{\mathrm{water/ethanol}}=\frac{\gamma_{\mathrm{water}}/\gamma_{\mathrm{ethanol}}}{X_{\mathrm{water}}/X_{\mathrm{ethanol}}}}}\end{array}
$$
where $\boldsymbol{\W}$ is the total weight of the permeate $(\mathsf{k g})$ , A is the separation area of MOF thin film $(\mathsf{m}^{2})$ , t is the collecting time (h), $x$ and $\gamma$ are the weight fraction of components in the feed and permeate, respectively. The weight of permeate was calculated by measuring the mass increase of the cold trap after collection, and the mass fraction of alcohol and water in the feed and permeate were analyzed by gas chromatography (Ruihong Technologies, SP-7890) equipped with a packed column (PQ, $2\ m\times4\ m\ m_{,}$ ) and a thermal conductivity detector (TCD). The mass fraction of MeOH and MTBE in the feed and permeate were analyzed by gas chromatography (Ruihong Technologies, SP-7890) equipped with a flame ionization detector (FID). A commercial PEG-20  M capillary column $(30~\mathrm{m}~\mathsf{l e n g t h}\times0.25~\mathsf{m m}$ i.d.) was employed for analyses. The pervaporation performance of the porous $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ supports with/without $50\ n m$ Stöber silica was also investigated as control experiments where large fluxes with no obvious separation ability were observed for alcohol dehydration and MeOH/MTBE separation (Table S5, Supporting Information).
Characterizations: The morphology of MOF thin films was observed by field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM, Apreo S LoVac) operating at $5\ \mathsf{k V}.$ The cross-section ${\bf\Xi}(5~\upmu\mathsf{m}$ long, $3~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ high, ${\approx}70~\mathsf{n m}$ thick) in Figure $2g$ and $5h$ were prepared by a focused ion beam (FEI Helios NanoLab 460HP) using $30~\mathsf{k V}$ Ga ion, followed by further treatment to reduce the thickness of the specimen. High-angle annular dark field–scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) data were obtained using an FEI-Talos $\mathsf{F200}\mathsf{X}$ STEM where the spatial distribution of elements and line scan along the depth of the film was collected. The crystallinity of the MOFs thin films was determined by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Smartlab, SAXS, mode: In-Plane) using CuKa1 radiation (60 kV, 220 mA, $\lambda=0.754056~\mathsf{n m}_{\cdot}$ ) with $2\theta$ angle ranging from $7^{\circ}$ to $30^{\circ}$ with a step size of $70^{\circ}\:\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ . The surface area, pore volume, and pore size distribution of NU-906 particles were calculated from ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms using Brunauer–Emmet–Teller (BET) method and density functional theory (DFT). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were collected using a TEM JEM-F200 at an acceleration voltage of $200~\mathsf{k V}$ and an emission current of $75~\upmu\up A$ . The roughness, topographic image and height profile of MOF thin films were tested by atomic force microscope (AFM, Bruker, Nano Surface, Multimode 8). The images were acquired using Scanasyst Air probes S3 with a nominal spring constant of 0.4 and $0.7~\mathsf{N}~\mathsf{m}^{-1}$ , respectively. Images were processed using NanoScope Analysis 1.9 software (Bruker). The hydrophilicity of MOF thin film surface was characterized by static contact angles (DATAPHYSICS, OCA15EC). The purity of TCPB- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\sf B r}_{2}$ ligand was analyzed by proton Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (1H NMR). Prior to the NMR characterization, MOF samples $(1~\mathsf{m g})$ were digested with NaOD solution (0.1 M in deuterium oxide, $750\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ). After sonication for $20~\mathsf{m i n}$ , another $500~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of deuterium oxide was added to the mixture to dilute the solution. Proton NMR spectra were collected on a Bruker Avance II $500~\mathsf{M H z}$ system equipped with DCH CryoProbeand automated with a BACS-60 autosampler. The infrared spectra of MOF thin films were recorded from 4000 to $500~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) BRUKER Vertex70.
